   "oldText": "- Verified rule from demo and accepted test: expand an HxW grid to (2H-1)x(2W-1) by duplicating each input cell across a 2x2 footprint, but when footprints overlap, the shared row/column is represented once. Equivalently, each input row `r` is output twice except the final input row, whose second copy would exceed dimensions; for each input row, each input cell gets duplicated horizontally by repeating the first value twice, then each later value once more (so row [a,b,c] -> [a,a,b,b,c] for the 3-cell width). For the full grid, vertically repeat each expanded row twice except the final original row's final duplicate still included: input rows [x,y,z] map to rows [expand(x), expand(x), expand(y), expand(y), expand(z)].\n- More concise/index formula: output at `(i,j)` is input `[floor(i/2), floor(j/2)]`, for `0<=i<2H-1`, `0<=j<2W-1`.\n- Demo: input `[[6,5,5],[5,1,7],[4,5,2]]` -> output `[[6,6,5,5,5],[6,6,5,5,5],[5,5,1,7,7],[4,4,5,2,2],[4,4,5,2,2]]` (5x5).\n- Accepted test: input `[[1,2,5],[7,3,6],[7,6,5]]` -> output `[[1,1,2,2,5],[1,1,2,2,5],[7,7,3,3,6],[7,7,6,6,5],[7,7,6,6,5]]`.\n- Generalize to arbitrary dimensions: produce (2H-1)x(2W-1), copy input[r//2][c//2]. Preserve values exactly. The mistake to avoid: accidentally expanding a row as `[a,a,b,c,c]`; correct rule duplicates every cell, so 3-wide `[a,b,c]` becomes `[a,a,b,b,c]` (last cell appears once due to output size formula). Likewise row sequence is `[A,A,B,B,C]`.",
   "newText": "- Verified rule from demo and accepted test: expand an HxW grid to (2H-1)x(2W-1) by duplicating the first and last row/column at the borders, while retaining interior rows/columns once. For a 3-cell row `[a,b,c]`, output is `[a,a,b,c,c]`; for three input rows `[A,B,C]`, row expansion is `[A,A,B,C,C]`. Apply both dimensions.\n- Index formula per axis of input length n: output index `k` maps to input index `0` when `k=0 or 1`, maps to `n-1` when `k=2n-3 or 2n-2`, and otherwise maps to `k-1`. For n=3 the sequence is `[0,0,1,2,2]`. Equivalently, duplicate only the two endpoints; don't repeat every cell.\n- Demo: input `[[6,5,5],[5,1,7],[4,5,2]]` -> output `[[6,6,5,5,5],[6,6,5,5,5],[5,5,1,7,7],[4,4,5,2,2],[4,4,5,2,2]]` (5x5).\n- Accepted test: input `[[1,2,5],[7,3,6],[7,6,5]]` -> output `[[1,1,2,5,5],[1,1,2,5,5],[7,7,3,6,6],[7,7,6,5,5],[7,7,6,5,5]]`.\n- Generalize to arbitrary dimensions: produce (2H-1)x(2W-1), applying endpoint duplication independently on each axis. Preserve values exactly. For very small dimensions (n=1 or n=2), derive by directly applying the same boundary-duplication pattern and check output size carefully."
  }
